.TH HOST-METERING 1

.SH "NAME"
host-metering \- Host metering service

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B host-metering
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fICONFIG_FILE_PATH\fR]
.IR daemon

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.B host-metering
Host metering service regularly notifies remote server about the host's
CPU count together with subscription and cloud information.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
.BR \-\-config =\fICONFIG_FILE_PATH\fR
Path to configuration file.
Default is /etc/host-metering.conf

.SH "ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES"
.PP

\fBHTTP_PROXY\fR
Will be used as the proxy URL for HTTP requests when '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'
configures no proxy, unless the target matches NO_PROXY.

\fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR
Will be used as the proxy URL for HTTPS requests when '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'
configures no proxy, unless the target matches NO_PROXY.

As systemd services do not have visibility into environment variables that are active in the user environment by default, the HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY variables set within the user environment will not be detected by the host-metering service.
The service uses the proxy configured for subscription-manager: the 'proxy_hostname', 'proxy_port', 'proxy_user' (or 'proxy_username'), 'proxy_password' and 'no_proxy' values of the [server] section in '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'.
That proxy takes priority over HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY, mirroring subscription-manager: the variables are used only when '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf' configures no proxy, and setting just one of them never overrides the RHSM proxy for its scheme while the other stays unproxied or follows the environment.
The proxy is always addressed as http://HOST:PORT.
Hosts matching the RHSM 'no_proxy' list or the NO_PROXY variable are never proxied through it.
Localhost and loopback targets are never sent to the proxy read from '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'.
To use custom proxy settings that will persist across package updates, configure them for subscription-manager in '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'; a systemd drop-in under `/etc/systemd/system/host-metering.service.d/` only takes effect when that file configures no proxy, and an invalid proxy value there fails the request instead of silently going unproxied.

\fBNO_PROXY\fR
Specifies a string that contains comma-separated values
specifying hosts that should be excluded from proxying. Each value is
represented by an IP address prefix (1.2.3.4), an IP address prefix in
CIDR notation (1.2.3.4/8), a domain name, or a special DNS label (*).
An IP address prefix and domain name can also include a literal port
number (1.2.3.4:80).
A domain name matches that name and all subdomains. A domain name with
a leading "." matches subdomains only. For example "foo.com" matches
"foo.com" and "bar.foo.com"; ".y.com" matches "x.y.com" but not "y.com".
A single asterisk (*) indicates that no proxying should be done.
A best effort is made to parse the string and errors are
ignored.

More details can be found on the official go http documentation: https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#ProxyFromEnvironment
This variable excludes targets from both proxy sources: a target matched by NO_PROXY is sent to neither the HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY proxy nor the proxy read from '/etc/rhsm/rhsm.conf'.
The RHSM 'no_proxy' option uses the same syntax as described above.

\fBHOST_METERING_<OPTION>\fR
Every option of host-metering.conf(5) can be overridden through an
environment variable named \fBHOST_METERING_<OPTION>\fR in uppercase
(e.g. \fBHOST_METERING_WRITE_URL\fR). Environment variables are applied
last and take precedence over /etc/host-metering.conf; note that systemd
drop-in \fBEnvironment=\fR entries are such variables.

.SH "FILES"
.PP
\fI/etc/host-metering.conf\fR
.RS 4
Default configuration file.
.RE
.PP
\fI/var/run/host-metering\fR
.RS 4
The default directory for storing write ahead log files
.RE

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 if the command was successful

1 if an error occurred

2 if configuration is invalid

.PP
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR host-metering.conf(5)
